 :org/member-count-basis "source-pinned, with the decline as the point: membership peaked at 8.87 million in 1998 and had fallen to 4.38 million by 2022, while the 65+ population GREW from 20.51M to 36.23M — participation collapsed from 43% to 12% of the age group. A separate federation figure of 103,281 clubs / 5,879,616 members circulates and appears to be an older snapshot; both are recorded rather than silently reconciled (全国老人クラブ連合会 令和5年度 老人クラブ実態調査)."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/governance-model :democratic
 :org/mission-statement "Neighborhood clubs for older residents running exercise, hobby, traffic-safety and friendly-visit activities — the largest formal senior membership structure in Japan, and a rare case where a community form's decline is measured precisely enough to be undeniable."
 :org/coverage-note "Target population nearly doubled while membership halved. Aging societies do not automatically produce senior communities."
 :org/source-name "公益財団法人 全国老人クラブ連合会「老人クラブ実態調査報告書（令和5年度）」"
